{
  "id": "COMPOUND:34435 - PROTEIN:3706",
  "verbalization": "Ent-Gloeosteretriol is implicated in the regulation of Tyrosine-protein kinase JAK2 through a series of molecular interactions. The evidence suggests that Ent-Gloeosteretriol acts as a target for multiple compounds, which in turn target JAK2. Specifically, Ent-Gloeosteretriol is directly linked to Phosphatidylinositol 4-phosphate 3-kinase C2 domain-containing subunit beta, a protein that is a target of 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline, which itself targets JAK2. Similarly, Ent-Gloeosteretriol is also a target of Serine/threonine-protein kinase ICK and DMS-53, both of which are targets of Oblonganoside C and Gerontoisoflavone A, respectively, which also target JAK2. These findings suggest that Ent-Gloeosteretriol may serve as a central node in a network of interactions that ultimately lead to the activation or modulation of JAK2. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Ent-Gloeosteretriol to influence JAK2 through diverse molecular mechanisms, possibly involving phosphatidylinositol signaling or kinase activation. The evidence supports a role for Ent-Gloeosteretriol in the regulation of JAK2, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
